WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:07.923
A fraud job wants the first bad authorization out of a million: number 518.

2
00:00:07.923 --> 00:00:13.913
The risk model takes 2 milliseconds a call, yet a List chain calls it a million times.

3
00:00:13.913 --> 00:00:17.952
33 minutes, for an answer that was there after one second.

4
00:00:18.733 --> 00:00:21.229
To see why, shrink it to five payments.

5
00:00:21.229 --> 00:00:22.957
The green ones are settled.

6
00:00:22.957 --> 00:00:28.460
The chain keeps the settled ones, turns each into its amount, and takes the first two.

7
00:00:29.233 --> 00:00:35.633
On a List, each step runs over every payment, and builds a new list before the next step starts.

8
00:00:35.633 --> 00:00:38.633
filter checks all five, and keeps 1, 3 and 5.

9
00:00:39.167 --> 00:00:42.842
Then map runs on all three, and take keeps the first two.

10
00:00:42.842 --> 00:00:45.744
Eight calls, and a new list after every step.

11
00:00:45.744 --> 00:00:47.549
People call this horizontal.

12
00:00:48.567 --> 00:00:50.369
Now put asSequence in front.

13
00:00:50.369 --> 00:00:52.429
Building the chain runs nothing.

14
00:00:52.429 --> 00:00:55.390
The work starts when toList asks for a result.

15
00:00:55.390 --> 00:00:56.807
Which lines print now?

16
00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:03.150
take asks map, map asks filter, and filter reads payment 1.

17
00:01:03.150 --> 00:01:06.765
It passes, goes straight on to map, and lands in take.

18
00:01:06.967 --> 00:01:08.963
Payment 2 fails the filter.

19
00:01:08.963 --> 00:01:12.956
Payment 3 passes, is mapped, and take now has its two.

20
00:01:12.956 --> 00:01:13.843
So it stops.

21
00:01:13.843 --> 00:01:16.209
Payments 4 and 5 are never read.

22
00:01:16.433 --> 00:01:20.931
The same result, with five calls instead of eight, and no lists in between.

23
00:01:20.931 --> 00:01:24.169
This is vertical: one payment through the whole chain.

24
00:01:25.067 --> 00:01:26.770
Back on the million.

25
00:01:26.770 --> 00:01:29.667
The Sequence scores 518 and stops.

26
00:01:30.467 --> 00:01:33.698
One kind of step breaks the vertical flow: sorting.

27
00:01:33.698 --> 00:01:37.689
It can't hand out the smallest until it has seen every payment.

28
00:01:37.689 --> 00:01:43.137
So the first request pulls all five in, sorts them, and only then hands out payment 5.

29
00:01:44.933 --> 00:01:47.827
In Kotlin, the only change is asSequence.

30
00:01:47.827 --> 00:01:50.508
A Java Stream is lazy in the same way.

31
00:01:50.508 --> 00:01:54.319
Java has no eager chain: you collect after every step.

32
00:01:56.300 --> 00:01:59.448
So: a List finishes each step for every element.

33
00:01:59.448 --> 00:02:04.761
A Sequence moves one element through every step, and only as far as the end asks.

34
00:02:04.967 --> 00:02:05.925
A quick check.

35
00:02:05.925 --> 00:02:08.595
On the List, move take(2) to the front.

36
00:02:08.595 --> 00:02:10.512
How many filter lines print?

37
00:02:12.900 --> 00:02:14.939
Two: filter 1 and filter 2.

38
00:02:14.939 --> 00:02:18.865
take cut the list first, so the result is just 1500.
